<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viracocha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wiracocha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Viracocha was the creator god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> and other Andean peoples, believed to have risen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> and made the sun, moon, stars and the first humans. The Incas honoured him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> alongside the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, and the eighth Inca ruler took his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viracocha.jpg" alt="Viracocha" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Wiener · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2Wiener-Tintin-Dieu_Soleil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Viracocha is the creator and supreme deity of pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> and Inca mythology in the Andes. According to the myth he had human appearance and was generally considered bearded, and he ordered the construction of Tiwanaku. His epithets include Ticsi Viracocha, Contiti Viracocha and Viracocha Pachayachachi, meaning teacher of the world. For the Inca the Viracocha cult was more important than the sun cult, and he was followed in the pantheon by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, the Sun.</p><p>The meaning of the name is uncertain. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> tiqsi means origin or beginning, wira fat and qucha lake or sea. A reading as fat or foam of the sea has been discarded for grammatical reasons since at least Inca Garcilaso. Max Uhle proposed that the name could mean lake of origin and of the end of all things, and some linguists suggest a borrowing from Aymara Wila Quta, blood lake, from sacrifices of camelids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the myth recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-betanzos">Juan de Betanzos</a>, Viracocha rose from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>, or sometimes from the cave of Paqariq Tampu, in a time of darkness to bring forth light. He made the sun, moon and stars. His first creation, brainless giants, displeased him and he destroyed them with a flood. He then made humans from smaller stones by breathing into them and scattered them across the world.</p><p>He wandered the earth disguised as a beggar, teaching the basics of civilisation and working miracles, and many refused his teachings. In the end he disappeared across the Pacific by walking on the water and was expected to reappear in times of trouble. In another legend a flood called Unu Pachakuti lasted 60 days and 60 nights and he saved two beings, Manco Cápac and Mama Uqllu, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> civilisation carrying a golden staff.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Spanish chroniclers equated Viracocha with the creator. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> wrote that the word means creator of all things, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-betanzos">Juan de Betanzos</a> wrote that Viracocha may be said to be God. Using the word God in place of Viracocha is seen as the first step in evangelising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>.</p><p>The chroniclers also described Viracocha as a white, bearded god, first done by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-cieza-de-leon">Pedro Cieza de León</a> in 1553. The whiteness is not mentioned in the authentic native legends of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>, and most modern scholars have considered the white god story a post-conquest Spanish invention. Bearded men appear on Moche pottery made long before the Spanish arrived. Local legend at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ollantaytambo">Ollantaytambo</a> sees a 140-metre rock formation on Cerro Pinkuylluna as a figure of Wiracochan, a messenger of Viracocha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viracocha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion centred on the sun god Inti, honoured at the Coricancha in Cusco, alongside the creator <b>Viracocha</b>, local sacred places called huacas, and the mummies of past rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Incas placed their own origins at the lake and built shrines on the Island of the Sun and the Island of the Moon, and in some versions of the creation story recorded by the Spaniards the god <b>Viracocha</b> made the first peoples at Tiwanaku.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tiwanaku">Tiwanaku · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the official deities were <b>Viracocha</b>, creator of humanity and everything else; Inti, the sun god, whose temple was the Coricancha, the Golden Enclosure; Illapa, who controlled weather, rain and lightning; Mamaquilla, Mother Moon and wife of Inti; Pachamama, the Earth Mother, protector of fields and crops; and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/inca-religion">Inca religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Under him, priests served the Sun, the Moon, the Thunder and the creator god <b>Viracocha</b> in Cusco, above all at the Coricancha, the gold-clad temple of the Sun, and at the state temples the Incas built in conquered provinces, while every people kept its own priests for its…</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li></ul></section>
